// fixtures.test.maxon — the shared half of `maxon fmt`'s gate: where the corpus is, how one case is
// staged and run, and the two things asked of the corpus itself. The per-case `test` declarations are
// the files beside this one, named for the PROPERTY each case gates.
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHAT THIS PROJECT PROVES: `fmt`'s product is a REPORT *and* A TREE, and neither one alone is
// the answer.** stdout says which files were written; it never says what. A run that reports
// `formatted: A.maxon` and writes garbage into it is byte-identical, on stdout, to the correct run.
// So every case here asserts four things — the exit status, stderr, stdout, and the whole resulting
// tree compared BOTH ways — and `tests/README.md` states the hazard each of those answers.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE EXPECTATIONS ARE THE BOOTSTRAP'S REAL ANSWERS, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THIS A PARITY GATE.**
// `tests/fmt/generate-expectations.py` mints every `expected-*` file and every `expected-tree/` by
// RUNNING `bin/maxon fmt`. A hand-written fixture proves only "shv2 does what I wrote down"; a
// generated one proves "shv2 does what the bootstrap does", which is the whole contract for a port.
// Never hand-edit an expectation; re-run the generator.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE WORKING DIRECTORY OF THE SPAWNED `fmt` IS THE STAGED TREE, NEVER THE CHECKOUT ROOT.**
// This inverts `maxon-shv2/Testing/test-command/`'s contract deliberately, and it is the one rule here
// whose violation would do damage rather than report a wrong answer: `flag-refused` and
// `flag-refused-no-path` exist because a regression makes `fmt` fall back to the CURRENT DIRECTORY and
// rewrite it in place — the incident that once rewrote 92 files across two agent worktrees. Run from
// the checkout root, those two fixtures would REPRODUCE it.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THIS FILE'S OWN PATHS, on the other hand, are relative to the CHECKOUT ROOT**, because that is
// what a test binary inherits from `maxon test` (`TestExecutor.launchTests` passes
// `inheritedWorkingDir()` and the command never chdirs). The two contracts live side by side: the
// HARNESS reads the corpus relative to the root, and the CHILD is given the staged tree. Everything
// handed to the child — the compiler path and the staged directory — is made absolute first, because a
// relative program path resolves against the child's new working directory on POSIX and against the
// parent's on Windows, and a gate must not depend on which.
//
// ⛔⛔ **ONE SPAWNING `test` PER FILE, AND THAT IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN TIDINESS.** A test FILE is
// the unit one process runs (`TestExecutor.shardsOf` puts a whole file in one launch) and that process
// has a 5000 ms default deadline. Every test here spawns the compiler TWICE — once for the answer and
// once for idempotence — so a file holding several of them reports a spurious TIMED OUT. Splitting by
// case makes each process hold one case, which no deadline can catch.
//
// ⚠ **NOTHING IS STAGED UNDER `tests/`.** The corpus is inert on disk on purpose (rule 1: no stored
// file is a live `.maxon`), and staging a runnable copy beside it would hand `maxon fmt` over the
// checkout exactly the live tree that rule exists to deny it. Staging goes to gitignored `temp/`.

// Where the corpus lives, and this project's own directory, both relative to the checkout root.
let FixturesDirName = "tests/fmt/fixtures"
let ProjectDirName = "tests/fmt"

// The one file in this directory that declares the shared half rather than a case. Together with the
// directory above it is how the coverage guard counts case files without a hand-maintained number.
let SharedHelperFileName = "fixtures.test.maxon"

// Where a case's runnable copy is built. Gitignored, outside `tests/`, and one directory per case so
// two cases can never see each other's tree.
let StagingRootDirName = "temp/fmt-fixtures"

// The binary under test, relative to the checkout root, without its host suffix.
let TestedCompilerStem = "maxon-shv2/.maxon/maxon-shv2"

// The command being exercised. `fmt` takes no options at all — that refusal is itself under test — so
// there is no reproducibility flag to add here the way `test`'s corpus needs two.
let TestedCommandName = "fmt"

// What a case directory holds. `input/` and `expected-tree/` are trees; the three `expected-*` files
// are the streams and the status. `argv.txt` is OPTIONAL, and an absent OR EMPTY one means the case
// passes no arguments — which is not a half-written fixture here but a case in its own right, since
// `fmt` with no path defaults to the working directory.
let InputDirName = "input"
let ExpectedTreeDirName = "expected-tree"
let ExpectedStdoutFileName = "expected-stdout.txt"
let ExpectedStderrFileName = "expected-stderr.txt"
let ExpectedExitFileName = "expected-exit.txt"
let ExtraArgvFileName = "argv.txt"

// ⛔ **THE STORED-NAME MAPPING, AND IT IS WRITTEN DOWN IN EXACTLY TWO PLACES THAT MUST AGREE** — here
// and in `generate-expectations.py`'s `stored_to_real` / `real_to_stored`. They stage the same trees,
// so a disagreement is silent: the generator would mint an expectation for a tree this harness never
// builds, and every case would fail for a reason having nothing to do with `fmt`.
//
//     <name>.fixture -> <name>        dot-<name> -> .<name>
//
// A DIRECTORY takes only the `dot-` half — appending `.fixture` to one would not round-trip.
let StoredFixtureSuffix = ".fixture"
let StoredDotPrefix = "dot-"

// The suffix that marks a source file as test code, and the plain one. The corpus guard below needs
// both: a `*.test.maxon` here is a driver and SHOULD be formatted like any other source; any other
// `.maxon` under this project would be a live source `maxon fmt` over the checkout would rewrite.
let TestSourceSuffix = ".test.maxon"
let MaxonSourceSuffix = ".maxon"

// The per-project build-products directory. The corpus walk refuses to descend into one: `maxon test`
// stages a COPY of this project under `tests/fmt/.maxon/test/staged/` and writes a generated entry
// source beside it, so a walk that went in would report the compiler's own output as a corpus offence.
let ProjectProductsDirName = ".maxon"

// ⚠ **THE THREE SUBSTITUTIONS, WHICH MUST MATCH `generate-expectations.py`'s `normalize` EXACTLY** —
// CR, the host path separator, and the absolute staging path. That file carries the reasoning for each
// one; what matters here is that the harness performs the identical three against ITS OWN staging
// directory, in the same order (the tree is matched in its forward-slash form, so the separator has to
// go first). A divergence between the two is silent: every pin would be minted in a shape this file
// never produces.
let CarriageReturn = "\r"
let LineFeed = "\n"
let PortablePathSeparator = "/"
let StagingTreeToken = "<TREE>"

// ⭐ **THE SUMMARY LINE'S GRAMMAR, WRITTEN ONCE AND USED THREE WAYS** — to parse the first run's two
// counts, to validate that what was parsed reconstructs the line byte for byte, and to PREDICT the
// second run's line. A separate parser and a separate builder would be the same fact twice, and the
// prediction is the whole idempotence assertion.
let SummaryPrefixWord = "fmt:"
let SummaryFilesWord = "file(s)"
let SummaryChangedWord = "changed,"
let SummaryUnchangedWord = "unchanged."
let SummaryWordSeparator = " "
let SummaryWordCount = 6
let ChangedCountWordIndex = 1
let UnchangedCountWordIndex = 4

// What a second run must report changed: nothing. Named because it is the assertion, not a literal.
let IdempotentChangedCount = 0

// The status a run that did its job exits with. Every other value is a refusal, and a refusal is
// idempotent in a different way — see `expectIdempotent`.
let SuccessExitCode = 0

// Which of a case's two runs a message is about. A tree comparison happens twice and the two failures
// mean different things: the first says `fmt` wrote the wrong bytes, the second says a re-run touched
// a tree it had already finished with.
let FirstRunLabel = "the first run"
let SecondRunLabel = "the second run"

// What each of a case's three required pins holds, said in the words a reader would need in order to
// mint a missing one.
let StdoutPinHolds = "everything this case's run must write to stdout"
let StderrPinHolds = "everything this case's run must write to stderr"
let ExitPinHolds = "the status this case's run must exit with, as one integer"


// An exit code as a fixture states it and as a run produces it. `Subprocess`'s own `ExitInt` is
// file-private there, so this is the same range spelled where it is used.
typealias RunExitCode = int(i64.min to i64.max)

// A file count as the summary line states it. FULL WIDTH deliberately: it is parsed out of text, so
// the value is whatever the report said, and narrowing it here would turn a wrong report into a range
// trap instead of the assertion failure a reader can act on.
typealias FileTally = int(i64.min to i64.max)

// A list of paths one directory walk produced. Declared HERE rather than reached for: stdlib's own
// `FilePathArray` is a member of `Directory` and has no file-scope spelling.
typealias FixturePathArray = Array with FilePath

// The relative paths one tree holds, asked as a membership question rather than read as a list.
typealias RelativePathSet = Set with String

// ============================================================================================
// THE CORPUS ITSELF
// ============================================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **A CASE NOBODY INVOKED READS EXACTLY LIKE A CASE THAT PASSED.** A fixture directory declares
// nothing by existing, so a case this file never names simply does not run, and a run that skipped it
// reports precisely what a run that checked it and agreed reports. Asked once, of the corpus as a
// whole, because no individual case can see its own absence.
//
// ⚠ **IT READS THIS FILE'S OWN SOURCE, and that is what makes it survive the layout.** The guard it
// replaced counted `<case>.test.maxon` FILES against case directories, which said something only while
// there was one file per case; the moment they were batched into this file it would have compared 21
// directories against 0 files and had to be deleted or weakened. Matching on the `checkFixture("…")`
// call itself asks the question that actually matters — is this case INVOKED — and it keeps asking it
// however the tests are arranged.
test 'every fixture case is invoked by name'
	let source = try File.readText(readableFile("{ProjectDirName}/{SharedHelperFileName}")) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — this file could not read its own source at {ProjectDirName}/{SharedHelperFileName}. Paths here are relative to the CHECKOUT ROOT; run from there.")

	var uninvoked = StringArray.create()
	for caseName in fixtureCaseNames() 'eachCase'
		if not source.contains("checkFixture(\"{caseName}\")") 'neverInvoked'
			uninvoked.push(caseName)
		end 'neverInvoked'
	end 'eachCase'

	let listed = listedLines(uninvoked)
	try Expect.isEmpty(listed, message: "these case directories under {FixturesDirName} are never invoked from {SharedHelperFileName}. Each one needs a `test` that calls `checkFixture(\"<case>\")`; a fixture nothing runs proves nothing and reads exactly like one that passed.")
end 'every fixture case is invoked by name'

// ⛔⛔ **NOTHING STORED IN THIS CORPUS MAY BE A LIVE `.maxon`, AND THE HAZARD IS THE TOOL UNDER TEST
// REWRITING ITS OWN ORACLE.** `maxon fmt` over the checkout walks `tests/`. A real `Ugly.maxon` under
// `fixtures/` would therefore be FORMATTED by the very command this corpus exists to gate — silently
// re-blessing every expectation here, `already-formatted` included, which would then be green forever
// by construction. That is why every stored source carries the inert suffix and this harness maps the
// name back at staging time.
//
// ⚠ **THE USUAL CURE — a `.maxonignore` at the corpus root — IS FORBIDDEN HERE**, and for a reason
// this directory owns rather than one borrowed from `test-command`: two of these fixtures
// (`maxonignore-pruned`, `maxonignore-above-root`) are ABOUT how `fmt` reads that marker, and a real
// one above them would hide the corpus from the walk under test as well as from the fixtures
// themselves. `tests/README.md` states it as a rule of the directory.
//
// The `*.test.maxon` drivers are exempt: they are ordinary sources that SHOULD be formatted, and both
// compilers' build walks drop them, so they are the one live thing that may sit here.
test 'no fixture is stored as a source maxon fmt would rewrite'
	let live = listedLines(liveSourcesUnder(readableDir(ProjectDirName)))
	try Expect.isEmpty(live, message: "these files under {ProjectDirName} are LIVE Maxon sources, so `maxon fmt` over this checkout would rewrite them — and under {FixturesDirName} that means the tool under test editing its own oracle. Store a fixture source as `<name>{StoredFixtureSuffix}`; do NOT add a `.maxonignore`, which would hide the corpus from the command it exists to gate and break the two `maxonignore` cases outright.")
end 'no fixture is stored as a source maxon fmt would rewrite'

// ⛔⛔ **AN OPTION TYPED AHEAD OF THE WORD `fmt` MUST STILL BE REFUSED — AND THIS IS THE ONLY CASE
// THAT CAN SAY SO.** Every fixture above goes through `FixtureCase.spawn`, which builds its argv as
// `fmt <extras>`; a flag can therefore only ever appear AFTER the command word there. But this
// driver's parser is ORDER-INDEPENDENT — one loop over every argument, arms in no particular
// relation to position — so `--emit-ir fmt` is a spelling the corpus above structurally cannot
// reach.
//
// MEASURED, and it is the incident the whole refusal exists for: before `MaxonArgs.anyOption`
// existed, `maxon-shv2 --emit-ir fmt` printed `formatted: <cwd>/U.maxon`, exited 0, and REWROTE THE
// WORKING DIRECTORY. The first fix was a `command == fmt` arm, which cannot see a flag that arrived
// before the command set it — so the record moved above the arm chain, where no ordering reaches
// around it.
//
// ⚠ **IT ASSERTS THE SAFETY PROPERTY, NOT THE MESSAGE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.** Every other pin in
// this corpus is the BOOTSTRAP's own answer (`generate-expectations.py`), which is what makes the
// corpus a parity oracle rather than a record of what its author expected. There is no reference
// answer for THIS spelling: the bootstrap dispatches positionally, so it reads `--emit-ir` as the
// command name and prints its top-level usage — a different grammar, pre-existing, and nothing this
// port introduced. Rather than hand-write an expectation and quietly weaken that rule, this asks the
// two things that actually matter and that hold for any wording: it must NOT succeed, and it must
// NOT have written anything.
test 'an option ahead of the command word is still refused'
	let staged = writableDir("{StagingRootDirName}/option-ahead-of-command")
	purgeFilesIn(staged)
	copyStagedTree(readableDir("{FixturesDirName}/flag-refused-implemented-option/{InputDirName}"), into: staged)

	let before = fileBody(staged.join("Ugly.maxon"))

	var arguments = StringArray.create()
	arguments.push("--emit-ir")
	arguments.push(TestedCommandName)
	let run = spawnCompiler(arguments, workingDirectory: staged, what: "the option-ahead-of-command probe")

	try Expect.notEqual(run.exitCode(), expected: 0, message: "`--emit-ir fmt` SUCCEEDED. An option this driver implements was swallowed by its own arm before `fmt` could refuse it, so `fmt` ran on a command line whose flag went unread — which for this command means rewriting a directory in place. See MaxonArgs.anyOption.")

	let after = fileBody(staged.join("Ugly.maxon"))
	try Expect.equal(after, expected: before, message: "`--emit-ir fmt` was refused but WROTE ANYWAY. The refusal has to happen before the walk, not after it: a non-zero status over an already-rewritten tree is the incident with a better exit code.")
end 'an option ahead of the command word is still refused'

// ============================================================================================
// ONE CASE
// ============================================================================================

// One case and everything an assertion about it needs. It exists so that the four things that travel
// together — the name a message quotes, the directory the pins live in, the tree that was staged, and
// that tree's portable spelling, which is what `<TREE>` stands for in a pin — cannot be passed
// separately and get out of step.
type FixtureCase
	let name as String
	let dir as FilePath
	let staged as FilePath
	let tree as String

	// Build a runnable copy of this case's `input/` under `temp/`, applying the stored-name mapping.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE STAGING DIRECTORY IS PURGED OF FILES RATHER THAN DELETED, because stdlib has no
	// directory removal.** That is enough for a re-run: a case's input tree has the same SHAPE every
	// time, so the directories left behind are exactly the ones staging is about to recreate, and
	// every file in them is written fresh. What it does not survive is a fixture whose input LOSES a
	// directory — an emptied `.git` left from a previous run would still prune a walk. Delete the
	// staging root by hand after removing one.
	static function stage(caseName String) returns FixtureCase
		let caseDir = readableDir("{FixturesDirName}/{caseName}")
		let staged = writableDir("{StagingRootDirName}/{caseName}")
		purgeFilesIn(staged)
		copyStagedTree(readableDir("{FixturesDirName}/{caseName}/{InputDirName}"), into: staged)

		return FixtureCase{name: caseName, dir: caseDir, staged: staged, tree: portableSpelling(staged)}
	end 'stage'

	// ⚠ **OBSERVED STREAMS AND COMMITTED PINS GO THROUGH THE IDENTICAL FUNCTION, AND THAT IS THE
	// POINT.** A correctly minted pin is already in this form, so folding it again changes nothing —
	// but two spellings of one normalization is exactly the shape that drifts, and the CR fold also
	// defends against a checkout that handed the pin back with Windows endings.
	function normalized(text String) returns String
		let folded = text.replace(CarriageReturn, with: "").replace(FilePath.separator(), with: PortablePathSeparator)
		let anonymized = folded.replace(self.tree, with: StagingTreeToken)
		if anonymized.isEmpty() or anonymized.endsWith(LineFeed) 'alreadyNormalized'
			return anonymized
		end 'alreadyNormalized'

		return "{anonymized}{LineFeed}"
	end 'normalized'

	// One of this case's three REQUIRED pins. Its absence is the instrument being broken, never a
	// pass: a case directory with no pin is a fixture somebody started and did not finish, and a sweep
	// that skipped it reports exactly what a sweep that checked it and agreed reports. `holds` is what
	// the missing file would have said, which is what a reader needs in order to mint it.
	function pinned(fileName String, holds String) returns String
		let path = self.dir.join("{fileName}")
		if not File.exists(path) 'noPin'
			panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — the '{self.name}' case has no {fileName}, so nothing says {holds}. Mint it with `python tests/fmt/generate-expectations.py {self.name}`; a case directory with no expectation proves nothing, and reads exactly like one that passed.")
		end 'noPin'

		let text = try File.readText(path) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — the '{self.name}' case's {fileName} exists but could not be read.")
		return normalized(text)
	end 'pinned'

	// The status this case's run must produce.
	function expectedExit() returns RunExitCode
		let text = pinned(ExpectedExitFileName, holds: ExitPinHolds)
		return try int.fromString(text.trim()) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — the '{self.name}' case's {ExpectedExitFileName} is not an integer. It must hold exactly the number the run exits with: {SuccessExitCode} when `fmt` did its job, 1 when it refused.")
	end 'expectedExit'

	// This case's own arguments, one per line of its `argv.txt`. Blank lines are dropped and each
	// argument is trimmed, so a checkout that handed back CRLF cannot turn `--check` into `--check\r`
	// — an argument `fmt` would refuse for a reason having nothing to do with the case.
	//
	// ⚠ An ABSENT file and an EMPTY one both mean "no arguments", and unlike `test-command`'s corpus
	// that is not a half-written fixture: `no-argument-defaults-to-cwd` is precisely the case where
	// `fmt` is given nothing and must fall back to the working directory.
	function argv() returns StringArray
		var extras = StringArray.create()

		let path = self.dir.join("{ExtraArgvFileName}")
		if not File.exists(path) 'takesNoArguments'
			return extras
		end 'takesNoArguments'

		let text = try File.readText(path) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — the '{self.name}' case has an {ExtraArgvFileName} that could not be read. A case whose arguments cannot be recovered would be run as some OTHER case, silently.")

		for line in text.split(LineFeed) 'eachLine'
			let argument = line.trim()
			if argument.isEmpty() 'blankLine'
				continue
			end 'blankLine'

			extras.push(argument)
		end 'eachLine'

		return extras
	end 'argv'

	// Spawn the compiler under test at this case's staged tree.
	//
	// ⛔ **EVERYTHING THAT WOULD STOP THE CASE RUNNING AT ALL PANICS RATHER THAN FAILING AN
	// ASSERTION.** A missing binary, an unspellable path, a spawn that never happened — none of those
	// is `fmt` answering wrongly, and a report that made them read alike would send a reader looking
	// for a defect in the thing that never ran.
	function spawn() returns CollectedOutput
		var arguments = StringArray.create()
		arguments.push(TestedCommandName)
		for extra in argv() 'eachExtra'
			arguments.push(extra)
		end 'eachExtra'

		return spawnCompiler(arguments, workingDirectory: self.staged, what: "the '{self.name}' case")
	end 'spawn'

	// The three streams a case pins, asserted against one run.
	function checkStreamsAgainstPins(run CollectedOutput, runLabel String) throws TestFailure
		let expectedStdout = pinned(ExpectedStdoutFileName, holds: StdoutPinHolds)
		let expectedStderr = pinned(ExpectedStderrFileName, holds: StderrPinHolds)

		try checkStreams(run, expectedStdout: expectedStdout, expectedStderr: expectedStderr, runLabel: runLabel)
	end 'checkStreamsAgainstPins'

	// ⭐⭐ **RUNNING `fmt` AGAIN MUST CHANGE NOTHING, AND "NOTHING" IS SPELLED TWO DIFFERENT WAYS.**
	//
	// A case that SUCCEEDED reports a summary whose two counts have MOVED: everything the first run
	// changed is unchanged the second time, so the total the walk sees is the same and the changed
	// count is zero. That prediction is derived from the first run's own pin rather than written into
	// each fixture, so it cannot drift case by case and a new fixture gets it for free.
	//
	// A case that was REFUSED changed nothing to begin with, so its second run must say exactly what
	// its first run said — the same pins, byte for byte. Predicting a summary line there would be
	// wrong: a refusal prints no summary at all.
	function checkIdempotence(run CollectedOutput) throws TestFailure
		if expectedExit() != SuccessExitCode 'aRefusal'
			try checkStreamsAgainstPins(run, runLabel: SecondRunLabel)
			return
		end 'aRefusal'

		let firstStdout = pinned(ExpectedStdoutFileName, holds: StdoutPinHolds)
		let seen = totalFilesSeen(firstStdout, caseName: self.name)
		let expectedStdout = "{summaryLine(IdempotentChangedCount, unchanged: seen)}{LineFeed}"
		let expectedStderr = pinned(ExpectedStderrFileName, holds: StderrPinHolds)

		try checkStreams(run, expectedStdout: expectedStdout, expectedStderr: expectedStderr, runLabel: SecondRunLabel)
	end 'checkIdempotence'

	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY HALF OF ONE RUN, IN THE ORDER A READER NEEDS THEM.** The status comes first
	// because it is the half a script reads; stderr comes before stdout because every `fmt` refusal
	// prints to stderr and NOTHING to stdout, so on a refusal case stderr is the payload and stdout is
	// empty on both sides. ONE place, so that order and the sentence explaining each question exist
	// once rather than once per caller.
	function checkStreams(run CollectedOutput, expectedStdout String, expectedStderr String, runLabel String) throws TestFailure
		let observedExit = run.exitCode()
		let expectedStatus = expectedExit()
		let observedStderr = normalized(run.stderr)
		try Expect.equal(observedExit, expected: expectedStatus, message: "'{self.name}' exited differently from its {ExpectedExitFileName} on {runLabel}. The status is the half of this report a SCRIPT reads, and the half that decides whether a refusal stops anything. It wrote to stderr: {observedStderr}")

		try Expect.equal(observedStderr, expected: expectedStderr, message: "'{self.name}' wrote different stderr on {runLabel}. Every `fmt` refusal goes here and prints NOTHING to stdout, so on a refusal case this is the whole message.")

		let observedStdout = normalized(run.stdout)
		try Expect.equal(observedStdout, expected: expectedStdout, message: "'{self.name}' wrote different stdout on {runLabel}. The `formatted:` lines name every file written, and the summary counts what the walk FOUND — a `0 unchanged` proves an excluded file was never reached, where `1 unchanged` would mean it was reached and merely left alone.")
	end 'checkStreams'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE TREE IS COMPARED BOTH WAYS, AND THE SECOND DIRECTION IS THE ONE THAT FINDS THINGS.**
	// Every file `expected-tree/` names must exist with identical bytes — that is the obvious half — and
	// every file the staged tree holds must be named by `expected-tree/`, which is what makes an
	// UNEXPECTED write red even when stdout is perfect. A walk that formatted a file it should have pruned
	// reports nothing about it and changes it anyway.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CONTENTS ARE COMPARED RAW, WITH NONE OF `normalized`'s FOLDING.** These are source bytes,
	// not a report: a formatter that started writing CRLF, or that dropped a file's final newline, must be
	// caught rather than normalized away.
	function checkTree(runLabel String) throws TestFailure
		let expectedTree = readableDir("{self.dir.toString()}/{ExpectedTreeDirName}")

		var missing = StringArray.create()
		var different = StringArray.create()
		var expectedPaths = RelativePathSet.create()

		for storedRelative in relativeFilePathsUnder(expectedTree, prefix: "") 'eachExpected'
			let relative = storedPathToReal(storedRelative)
			expectedPaths.insert(relative)

			let stagedFile = joinRelative(self.staged, relative: relative)
			if not File.exists(stagedFile) 'notWritten'
				missing.push(relative)
				continue
			end 'notWritten'

			let want = fileBody(joinRelative(expectedTree, relative: storedRelative))
			let got = fileBody(stagedFile)
			if want != got 'differentBytes'
				different.push(relative)
			end 'differentBytes'
		end 'eachExpected'

		var unexpected = StringArray.create()
		for relative in relativeFilePathsUnder(self.staged, prefix: "") 'eachStaged'
			if not expectedPaths.contains(relative) 'notInTheOracle'
				unexpected.push(relative)
			end 'notInTheOracle'
		end 'eachStaged'

		let report = treeReport(missing, different: different, unexpected: unexpected)
		try Expect.isEmpty(report, message: "'{self.name}' left a tree that does not match its {ExpectedTreeDirName} after {runLabel}. MISSING is a file `fmt` should have left in place or written; DIFFERENT is one it wrote the wrong bytes into; UNEXPECTED is one it wrote that the oracle does not name at all — the direction stdout can never report, because a run that formats a file it should have pruned says nothing about it.")
	end 'checkTree'
end 'FixtureCase'

// ⭐⭐ **ONE CASE, RUN TWICE.** The first run is compared against everything the fixture pins; then
// the whole thing is run AGAIN on the tree the first run left, and required to change nothing.
// Idempotence is a property no single-run fixture can see: a formatter rule that flips a file between
// two spellings formats correctly, reports correctly, and is still broken.
//
// The tree is compared after EACH run, and the two failures mean different things: the first says
// `fmt` wrote the wrong bytes, the second says a re-run touched a tree it had already finished with.
function checkFixture(caseName String) throws TestFailure
	let fixture = FixtureCase.stage(caseName)

	let firstRun = fixture.spawn()
	try fixture.checkStreamsAgainstPins(firstRun, runLabel: FirstRunLabel)
	try fixture.checkTree(FirstRunLabel)

	let secondRun = fixture.spawn()
	try fixture.checkIdempotence(secondRun)
	try fixture.checkTree(SecondRunLabel)
end 'checkFixture'

// The binary under test, absolute and with the suffix this host can actually spawn. ABSOLUTE because
// the child is given a different working directory: a relative program path resolves against the
// child's new directory on POSIX and against the parent's on Windows.
function testedCompilerPath() returns FilePath
	#if os(Windows)
		let spelled = "{TestedCompilerStem}.exe"
	#else
		let spelled = "{TestedCompilerStem}"
	#endif

	let relative = try FilePath.from(spelled) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — '{TestedCompilerStem}' is not a spellable path")
	return relative.resolve(Directory.currentPath())
end 'testedCompilerPath'

// ============================================================================================
// THE SUMMARY LINE
// ============================================================================================

// The line every successful `fmt` run ends with, built from the one grammar this file holds.
function summaryLine(changed FileTally, unchanged FileTally) returns String
	return "{SummaryPrefixWord} {changed} {SummaryFilesWord} {SummaryChangedWord} {unchanged} {SummaryUnchangedWord}"
end 'summaryLine'

// How many files a run's walk FOUND, read off its summary: everything it changed plus everything it
// left alone. That total is what the next run must report as unchanged.
//
// ⚠ **THE PARSE VALIDATES ITSELF BY REBUILDING THE LINE.** Two numbers lifted out by position could
// have come from a line of some other shape entirely; reconstructing the whole line from them and
// requiring it back byte for byte means the grammar is checked with the same spelling that produced
// it.
function totalFilesSeen(stdout String, caseName String) returns FileTally
	let summary = lastNonBlankLine(stdout)
	let words = summary.split(SummaryWordSeparator)
	if words.count() != SummaryWordCount 'wrongWordCount'
		panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — the '{caseName}' case's {ExpectedStdoutFileName} ends with '{summary}', which is not a `fmt` summary line. Every successful run ends with one, and the second run's expectation is derived from its two counts.")
	end 'wrongWordCount'

	let changed = countWordAt(words, index: ChangedCountWordIndex, summary: summary, caseName: caseName)
	let unchanged = countWordAt(words, index: UnchangedCountWordIndex, summary: summary, caseName: caseName)
	let rebuilt = summaryLine(changed, unchanged: unchanged)
	if rebuilt != summary 'wrongShape'
		panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — the '{caseName}' case's summary line reads '{summary}' where this file's grammar builds '{rebuilt}'. The two must agree: one grammar parses the first run and predicts the second.")
	end 'wrongShape'

	return changed + unchanged
end 'totalFilesSeen'

// One of the summary line's two counts.
function countWordAt(words StringArray, index FileTally, summary String, caseName String) returns FileTally
	let word = try words.get(index) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — the '{caseName}' case's summary line '{summary}' has no word {index}.")
	return try int.fromString(word) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — the '{caseName}' case's summary line '{summary}' holds '{word}' where a file count belongs.")
end 'countWordAt'

// The last line of a stream that says anything. `fmt` ends every successful run with its summary and
// then a newline, so the trailing empty piece of the split is not the line anybody means.
function lastNonBlankLine(text String) returns String
	var last = ""
	for line in text.split(LineFeed) 'eachLine'
		let candidate = line.trim()
		if not candidate.isEmpty() 'saysSomething'
			last = candidate
		end 'saysSomething'
	end 'eachLine'

	return last
end 'lastNonBlankLine'

// ============================================================================================
// STAGING
// ============================================================================================

// Copy one stored tree into a runnable one, mapping every name on the way.
function copyStagedTree(stored FilePath, into FilePath)
	for entry in listing(stored) 'eachEntry'
		let target = into.join("{storedToReal(entry.filename())}")
		if Directory.isDirectory(entry) 'aDirectory'
			if not Directory.create(target) 'couldNotCreate'
				panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — could not create the staging directory {target.toString()}.")
			end 'couldNotCreate'

			copyStagedTree(entry, into: target)
			continue
		end 'aDirectory'

		let body = try File.readText(entry) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — could not read the stored fixture file {entry.toString()}.")
		try File.writeText(target, content: body) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — could not stage {target.toString()}.")
	end 'eachEntry'
end 'copyStagedTree'

// ⛔ **THE STORED-NAME MAPPING — the harness half of the pair `generate-expectations.py` holds.** One
// function serves files and directories both: a directory carries only the `dot-` half, and a name
// carrying neither marker is already what it will be called.
function storedToReal(stored String) returns String
	var real = stored
	if real.endsWith(StoredFixtureSuffix) 'inertSource'
		let suffixStart = try real.findLast(StoredFixtureSuffix) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — '{real}' ends with {StoredFixtureSuffix} and yet does not contain it.")
		real = real.slice(real.startIndex(), endIndex: suffixStart)
	end 'inertSource'

	if real.startsWith(StoredDotPrefix) 'hiddenName'
		real = ".{real.replaceFirst(StoredDotPrefix, with: "")}"
	end 'hiddenName'

	return real
end 'storedToReal'

// The same mapping applied to a whole relative path, so a stored tree's walk can be compared against a
// staged tree's walk without staging anything twice.
function storedPathToReal(stored String) returns String
	var real = ""
	var atFirstComponent = true
	for component in stored.split(PortablePathSeparator) 'eachComponent'
		let mapped = storedToReal(component)
		if atFirstComponent 'firstComponent'
			real = mapped
			atFirstComponent = false
			continue
		end 'firstComponent'

		real = "{real}{PortablePathSeparator}{mapped}"
	end 'eachComponent'

	return real
end 'storedPathToReal'

// Delete every file under a directory, leaving the directories themselves. The same shape, for the
// same reason, as `SpecTestRunner.purgeFilesIn`.
function purgeFilesIn(dir FilePath)
	for entry in listing(dir) 'eachEntry'
		if Directory.isDirectory(entry) 'aDirectory'
			purgeFilesIn(entry)
			continue
		end 'aDirectory'

		try File.delete(entry) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — could not clear {entry.toString()} from a previous run's staging tree. Left there, it would be compared as though `fmt` had written it.")
	end 'eachEntry'
end 'purgeFilesIn'

// A path as the pins spell one: forward slashes whatever the host uses.
function portableSpelling(path FilePath) returns String
	return path.toString().replace(FilePath.separator(), with: PortablePathSeparator)
end 'portableSpelling'

// ============================================================================================
// COMPARING THE TREE
// ============================================================================================

// The three kinds of tree difference, rendered as the one text a reader has to act on. Empty when the
// trees agree, which is what makes `checkTree`'s assertion an `isEmpty` on the evidence itself.
function treeReport(missing StringArray, different StringArray, unexpected StringArray) returns String
	var report = ""
	report.append(listedUnder(missing, heading: "MISSING"))
	report.append(listedUnder(different, heading: "DIFFERENT"))
	report.append(listedUnder(unexpected, heading: "UNEXPECTED"))

	return report
end 'treeReport'

// One heading and its paths, or nothing at all when there are none.
function listedUnder(paths StringArray, heading String) returns String
	if paths.isEmpty() 'nothingToSay'
		return ""
	end 'nothingToSay'

	return "{LineFeed}{heading}:{listedLines(paths)}"
end 'listedUnder'

// Every file under `root`, as a path relative to it with `/` between the components whatever the host
// spells. The order is the directory listing's and nothing depends on it: these paths are compared as
// SETS, and their only ordered use is the offender list a failure message prints.
//
// Directories are descended into unconditionally — `.git` and `.maxonignore` included, since
// whether `fmt` respected those is exactly what the tree is being compared to find out.
function relativeFilePathsUnder(root FilePath, prefix String) returns StringArray
	var found = StringArray.create()

	for entry in listing(root) 'eachEntry'
		let name = entry.filename()
		let relative = name if prefix.isEmpty() else "{prefix}{PortablePathSeparator}{name}"
		if Directory.isDirectory(entry) 'aDirectory'
			for nested in relativeFilePathsUnder(entry, prefix: relative) 'eachNested'
				found.push(nested)
			end 'eachNested'
			continue
		end 'aDirectory'

		found.push(relative)
	end 'eachEntry'

	return found
end 'relativeFilePathsUnder'

// A relative path built by `relativeFilePathsUnder`, resolved against the root it came from.
//
// ⚠ **THE INTERPOLATION AROUND EVERY `join` ARGUMENT IN THIS FILE IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT NOISE.** The
// BOOTSTRAP reports `FilePath.join` as an ambiguous overload set (E3007, the `String` and `FilePath`
// candidates) for a plain `String` argument, where shv2 resolves it; these tests must compile under
// both, so they take the spelling both accept. Same divergence, same cure, as the locals `Expect.equal`
// is handed above.
function joinRelative(root FilePath, relative String) returns FilePath
	var path = root
	for component in relative.split(PortablePathSeparator) 'eachComponent'
		path = path.join("{component}")
	end 'eachComponent'

	return path
end 'joinRelative'

// One file's bytes as text. A file that cannot be read is the instrument being broken: both trees are
// on this machine and one of them was written moments ago.
function fileBody(path FilePath) returns String
	return try File.readText(path) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — could not read {path.toString()} while comparing a case's tree.")
end 'fileBody'

// ============================================================================================
// WALKING THE CORPUS
// ============================================================================================

// A directory this file's paths name, refused loudly when it is not there. ONE speller, because every
// such refusal is really the same one — the working-directory contract in the header was not met — and
// each site repeating it is how the sentence explaining it comes to exist in only some of them.
function readableDir(spelled String) returns FilePath
	let path = try FilePath.from(spelled) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — '{spelled}' is not a spellable path")
	if not Directory.isDirectory(path) 'notThere'
		panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — there is no directory at '{spelled}'. Every path in this file is relative to the CHECKOUT ROOT, which is the working directory a test binary inherits from `maxon test`; a run started anywhere else finds nothing.")
	end 'notThere'

	return path
end 'readableDir'

// A directory this run WRITES into, created if it is not there yet, and ABSOLUTE because the staging
// tree is handed to a child process as its working directory.
function writableDir(spelled String) returns FilePath
	let path = try FilePath.from(spelled) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — '{spelled}' is not a spellable path")
	if not Directory.create(path) 'couldNotCreate'
		panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — could not create '{spelled}'. It is where a case's runnable copy is built; nothing can be staged without it.")
	end 'couldNotCreate'

	return path.resolve(Directory.currentPath())
end 'writableDir'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ROSTER IS READ FROM THE FILESYSTEM, NEVER FROM A CONSTANT.** A hand-written list is a
// guard only while somebody maintains it: adding a fixture without adding its name would leave the
// case unrun, and an unrun case reads exactly like one that passed — the very thing the guard exists
// to close.
//
// Files sitting at the corpus root are not cases and are not listed: a note beside the directories
// (`census-sources.txt`) is not a fixture.
function fixtureCaseNames() returns StringArray
	var names = StringArray.create()

	for entry in listing(readableDir(FixturesDirName)) 'eachEntry'
		if Directory.isDirectory(entry) 'aCase'
			names.push(entry.filename())
		end 'aCase'
	end 'eachEntry'

	return names
end 'fixtureCaseNames'

// A file this harness must be able to read, resolved with the same loud failure `readableDir` gives a
// directory — and for its reason: every path here is relative to the CHECKOUT ROOT, so a run started
// anywhere else finds nothing and must say so rather than reporting an empty corpus as agreement.
function readableFile(spelled String) returns FilePath
	let path = try FilePath.from(spelled) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — '{spelled}' is not a spellable path")
	if not File.exists(path) 'notThere'
		panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — there is no file at '{spelled}'. Every path in this file is relative to the CHECKOUT ROOT, which is the working directory a test binary inherits from `maxon test`; a run started anywhere else finds nothing.")
	end 'notThere'

	return path
end 'readableFile'

// Every `.maxon` file under `root` that is not a test driver, skipping `.maxon/` product directories —
// `maxon test` stages a copy of this project there and writes a generated entry source beside it, so a
// walk that went in would report the compiler's own output as a corpus offence.
function liveSourcesUnder(root FilePath) returns StringArray
	var live = StringArray.create()

	for entry in listing(root) 'eachEntry'
		let name = entry.filename()
		if Directory.isDirectory(entry) 'aDirectory'
			if name == ProjectProductsDirName 'buildProducts'
				continue
			end 'buildProducts'

			for nested in liveSourcesUnder(entry) 'eachNested'
				live.push(nested)
			end 'eachNested'
			continue
		end 'aDirectory'

		if name.endsWith(MaxonSourceSuffix) and not name.endsWith(TestSourceSuffix) 'aLiveSource'
			live.push(entry.toString())
		end 'aLiveSource'
	end 'eachEntry'

	return live
end 'liveSourcesUnder'

// The paths a refusal lists, one per line — empty when there are none, which is what makes the
// assertions above an `isEmpty` on the very text a reader has to act on.
function listedLines(paths StringArray) returns String
	var listed = ""
	for path in paths 'eachPath'
		listed.append("{LineFeed}  {path}")
	end 'eachPath'

	return listed
end 'listedLines'

// What one directory holds. A listing that fails is the instrument being broken — the corpus is
// checked into this repository, and the staging tree was written by this process moments ago.
function listing(dir FilePath) returns FixturePathArray
	return try Directory.list(dir) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — could not list {dir.toString()}")
end 'listing'


// ============================================================================================
// THE CASES
// ============================================================================================

// ⚠ **ONE FILE, NOT ONE PER CASE — AND THE RULE IT DEPARTS FROM IS A DEADLINE, NOT A STYLE.**
// `maxon-shv2/Testing/test-command/` gives each spawning `test` its own file because a file is what
// ONE process runs and that process has a 5,000 ms deadline, and ITS fixtures each COMPILE A PROJECT
// at roughly half a second apiece. These format a tiny staged tree instead.
//
// MEASURED on this corpus, merged: **26 tests, 2,718 ms** against that deadline — 1.8x headroom, and
// the 22 case tests average 61 ms. Splitting them back out would ADD time, not save it: the only
// thing a split buys back is process startups, and they are not where the time goes.
//
// ⛔ **THE ONE CASE THAT COULD BRING THE DEADLINE BACK IS THE CENSUS**, which formats real compiler
// sources and costs ~1,270 ms on its own — roughly HALF the run. Its roster is short for that reason
// and its own header says so; `Parser.maxon` is deliberately off it at ~2.6 s per pass. If this file
// ever does time out, the answer is that roster or `--timeout=`, never a return to one file per case.
//
// ⭐ And one file is what keeps the constants and helpers below from being redeclared per case. They
// were, in the first draft — a separate census file carried its own `TestedCompilerStem`,
// `TestedCommandName`, `LineFeed`, `PortablePathSeparator` and `SuccessExitCode`. In one file those
// names COLLIDE, so the duplication is not discouraged here, it is impossible.

// already-formatted.test.maxon — a source already in the formatter's own output shape is
// REWRITTEN BY NOBODY. `0 changed, 1 unchanged` is the pair of numbers that says so: the walk
// found the file and decided against it, which is a different fact from never having found it.
//
test 'an already-formatted source is found and left alone'
	try checkFixture("already-formatted")
end 'an already-formatted source is found and left alone'

// build-manifest-case-folded.test.maxon — the build manifest is recognised WITHOUT REGARD TO
// CASE, so a `BUILD.maxon` is skipped exactly as `build.maxon` is.
//
// ⭐ `0 changed, 0 unchanged` is the load-bearing half: it says the walk never counted the file
// at all. `0 changed, 1 unchanged` would mean the manifest was found and merely left as it was,
// which is a different implementation passing the same stdout check.
//
test 'a manifest named BUILD.maxon is skipped like build.maxon'
	try checkFixture("build-manifest-case-folded")
end 'a manifest named BUILD.maxon is skipped like build.maxon'

// build-manifest-skipped.test.maxon — a directory walk formats the sources and never reaches the
// build manifest beside them. The manifest is a project declaration the compiler reads, not a
// source the formatter owns.
//
// ⭐ `1 changed, 0 unchanged` proves the skip happened in the WALK: had the manifest been visited
// and declined, it would be counted as unchanged.
//
test 'a directory walk formats the sources and never visits build.maxon'
	try checkFixture("build-manifest-skipped")
end 'a directory walk formats the sources and never visits build.maxon'

// command-word-as-path — ⛔⛔ **A DIRECTORY WHOSE NAME SPELLS A COMMAND IS STILL A PATH, AND THE
// SPELLING THAT FAILS IS `fmt fmt`.** This driver's argument parser is ORDER-INDEPENDENT — one loop,
// arms in no relation to position — so before `laterCommandWordIsAPath` existed the second `fmt` set
// the command a second time and was never filed as a positional. `runFmt` then saw NO path, took its
// working-directory default, and REWROTE THE WHOLE TREE in place: exit 0, `2 file(s) changed`, with a
// file the caller never named among them. That is the 92-file worktree incident reached through the
// POSITIONAL door, which every `flag-refused` case above guards only the FLAG door of.
//
// ⭐ `Outside.maxon` is stored deliberately UNFORMATTED and pinned that way: a regression formats it,
// and the tree comparison is the only thing that can say so — stdout on the broken run named it, but
// a run that named nothing and wrote nothing would read the same as this one.
//
// The expectation is the BOOTSTRAP's own answer, as every case here is: it dispatches on `args[0]` and
// hands the rest to `RunFmt`, so it cannot have this defect and is the right oracle for it.
//
test 'a directory whose name spells a command is still a path'
	try checkFixture("command-word-as-path")
end 'a directory whose name spells a command is still a path'

// directory-walk.test.maxon — a directory argument reaches every `.maxon` beneath it at every
// depth, and reports them in one stable order. The order is part of the pin: a walk whose output
// depends on the filesystem's listing order cannot be diffed between two compilers at all.
//
test 'a directory argument formats every source beneath it, at any depth'
	try checkFixture("directory-walk")
end 'a directory argument formats every source beneath it, at any depth'

// empty-directory.test.maxon — a tree holding no Maxon source at all is not an error and not a
// refusal: `fmt` reports that it found nothing and exits 0. The `notes.txt` in it is the point — a
// non-source file must be neither formatted nor counted.
//
test 'a tree with no Maxon source reports nothing found and succeeds'
	try checkFixture("empty-directory")
end 'a tree with no Maxon source reports nothing found and succeeds'

// flag-refused-implemented-option.test.maxon — ⛔⛔ **THE DANGEROUS HALF OF THE FLAG REFUSAL, AND THE
// ONE `flag-refused` CANNOT SEE.** That case passes `--check`, which NO command in this driver
// implements, so it is refused by the catch-all unknown-flag arm and would stay refused even if `fmt`
// had no rule of its own. This one passes `--emit-ir`, which `maxon-shv2` DOES implement — so an arm
// that understands it sets `emitIr`, leaves `badOption` empty, and lets `fmt` run on a command line
// whose flag was silently swallowed. For this command that means rewriting a directory in place.
//
// MEASURED before the fix: `maxon-shv2 --emit-ir fmt` printed `formatted: <cwd>/U.maxon` and exited 0.
// See `MaxonArgs.anyOption`, which records every `-`-leading argument ABOVE the arm chain so no arm can
// swallow one first, and `fixtures.test.maxon`'s `an option ahead of the command word is still refused`
// for the ORDERING half — the two together are what close it.
//
// The expectation here is the BOOTSTRAP's own answer: it refuses any `-`-leading argument in `RunFmt`,
// so both compilers print the identical two lines for this spelling.
//
test 'an option this driver implements is still refused rather than swallowed'
	try checkFixture("flag-refused-implemented-option")
end 'an option this driver implements is still refused rather than swallowed'

// flag-refused-no-path.test.maxon — the same refusal with NO path beside the option, which is the
// shape that actually falls back to the current directory when the guard is missing.
// `flag-refused` passes `.` as well, so a regression there could be masked by the path it was
// given; here there is no path at all, and the only two outcomes are a refusal or a whole-tree
// rewrite.
//
test 'an option with no path is refused rather than falling back to the working directory'
	try checkFixture("flag-refused-no-path")
end 'an option with no path is refused rather than falling back to the working directory'

// flag-refused.test.maxon — ⛔ **THE FIXTURE THAT GATES A DESTRUCTIVE REGRESSION.** `fmt` takes no
// options, and an unrecognized one must be REFUSED — with the usage line, on stderr, exit 1,
// nothing written. The failure this prevents is specific and it has happened: an unrecognized
// flag that matched no path once fell through to "format the current directory", and one
// accidental whole-tree run rewrote 92 files across two agent worktrees.
//
// ⚠ That is also why this harness runs every case with the STAGED TREE as the working directory.
// Run from the checkout root, this fixture would reproduce the incident instead of gating it.
//
test 'an unrecognized option is refused on stderr and nothing is written'
	try checkFixture("flag-refused")
end 'an unrecognized option is refused on stderr and nothing is written'

// git-at-root-not-pruned.test.maxon — ⭐⭐ **ONE HALF OF A PAIR THAT FAILS IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS,
// AND NEITHER HALF CONSTRAINS THE RULE ALONE.** Its partner is `nested-git-pruned`.
//
// A `.git` BELOW the traversal root marks a separate checkout and must prune that subtree
// (`nested-git-pruned`). A `.git` AT the traversal root is this checkout's own, and pruning on it
// would make `maxon fmt` inside any repository format NOTHING — so it must not prune.
//
// A rule that never prunes passes this case and fails its partner; a rule that always prunes
// passes its partner and fails this one. Only the two together say where the boundary is.
//
test 'a .git at the traversal root does not prune the walk'
	try checkFixture("git-at-root-not-pruned")
end 'a .git at the traversal root does not prune the walk'

// maxonignore-above-root.test.maxon — a `.maxonignore` does not stop applying because the walk was
// started BELOW it. The named root here is `vendor/deep` and the marker sits in `vendor/`: the
// subtree is still excluded, and `0 changed, 0 unchanged` says the walk found nothing rather than
// finding the file and declining it.
//
// ⚠ Read against `named-path-escapes-ignore`, which names a FILE under the same marker and DOES
// format it. Naming a file is an instruction; naming a directory is still a walk.
//
test 'a .maxonignore above the named directory still excludes it'
	try checkFixture("maxonignore-above-root")
end 'a .maxonignore above the named directory still excludes it'

// maxonignore-pruned.test.maxon — a `.maxonignore` prunes its whole subtree from a directory walk,
// however deep. `1 changed, 0 unchanged` is the assertion: the one source outside the marked
// directory is formatted and the two inside it are never counted at all.
//
test 'a .maxonignore prunes its entire subtree from a walk'
	try checkFixture("maxonignore-pruned")
end 'a .maxonignore prunes its entire subtree from a walk'

// named-path-escapes-ignore.test.maxon — a file named ON THE COMMAND LINE is formatted even though
// a `.maxonignore` beside it excludes the directory from a walk. The marker says what a SWEEP
// should skip; it is not a refusal to touch the file when somebody asks for it by name.
//
// ⚠ The partner case is `maxonignore-above-root`, which names the DIRECTORY instead and gets
// nothing.
//
test 'a file named on the command line is formatted despite a .maxonignore'
	try checkFixture("named-path-escapes-ignore")
end 'a file named on the command line is formatted despite a .maxonignore'

// named-path-escapes-manifest.test.maxon — the build manifest is skipped by the WALK, not exempted
// from formatting: named on the command line, `build.maxon` is formatted like any other source.
// The two facts are easy to conflate into one "manifests are never touched" rule, and this case
// is the half that would go red if they were.
//
test 'a build manifest named on the command line is formatted'
	try checkFixture("named-path-escapes-manifest")
end 'a build manifest named on the command line is formatted'

// nested-git-pruned.test.maxon — ⭐⭐ **ONE HALF OF A PAIR THAT FAILS IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS, AND
// NEITHER HALF CONSTRAINS THE RULE ALONE.** Its partner is `git-at-root-not-pruned`.
//
// A `.git` BELOW the traversal root marks a checkout that is not ours, and its subtree must be
// pruned — in BOTH of git's spellings, the `.git/` directory of a clone and the `.git` FILE of a
// worktree. This tree holds one of each, and only `Top.maxon` may be formatted.
//
// A rule that always prunes passes this case and fails its partner, where a `.git` at the root
// would make `maxon fmt` inside any repository format nothing. Only the two together say where
// the boundary is.
//
test 'a .git below the traversal root prunes its subtree, as a directory and as a file'
	try checkFixture("nested-git-pruned")
end 'a .git below the traversal root prunes its subtree, as a directory and as a file'

// no-argument-defaults-to-cwd.test.maxon — `fmt` with no path formats the WORKING DIRECTORY. That
// is the documented default, and it is the behaviour every flag-refusal case exists to keep from
// being reached by accident — so it has to be pinned somewhere, deliberately, as itself.
//
// ⚠ **THIS IS THE ONE CASE WHOSE OUTPUT IS ABSOLUTE**, because the default has no argument
// spelling to echo. The staging directory differs on every run, so the harness and
// `generate-expectations.py` both fold it to the literal `<TREE>` — the third of the three
// substitutions their `normalized` / `normalize` must agree on.
//
test 'no argument formats the working directory'
	try checkFixture("no-argument-defaults-to-cwd")
end 'no argument formats the working directory'

// path-not-found.test.maxon — a path that is not there is a refusal on stderr with exit 1, NOT a
// silent no-op and not a fallback to the working directory. A mistyped filename that formatted
// the whole tree instead is the same incident the flag-refusal cases gate, reached through a
// different door.
//
test 'a path that does not exist is refused, and the real source beside it is untouched'
	try checkFixture("path-not-found")
end 'a path that does not exist is refused, and the real source beside it is untouched'

// section-separator-file.test.maxon — a spec fragment named on the command line is formatted even
// though its extension is not `.maxon` and its body is three `---`-separated sections rather than
// one program. Naming a path is an instruction about THAT FILE; the extension is how a WALK
// decides.
//
test 'a named file is formatted whatever its extension'
	try checkFixture("section-separator-file")
end 'a named file is formatted whatever its extension'

// section-separator-not-walked.test.maxon — the other half of `section-separator-file`: the same
// fragment, in the same directory, is NEVER FOUND by a walk. `1 changed, 0 unchanged` says so —
// the fragment is not counted as unchanged, because the walk selects on the extension and never
// opens it.
//
test 'a walk selects on the extension and never finds a fragment beside a source'
	try checkFixture("section-separator-not-walked")
end 'a walk selects on the extension and never finds a fragment beside a source'

// single-file.test.maxon — a file argument formats THAT FILE and nothing else. The sibling source
// in the same directory is the assertion: it is not named, so it must come out byte-identical,
// and the tree comparison is what catches it if it does not — stdout would say nothing either
// way.
//
test 'a file argument formats only that file, leaving its sibling untouched'
	try checkFixture("single-file")
end 'a file argument formats only that file, leaving its sibling untouched'

// two-paths-refused.test.maxon — `fmt` takes AT MOST ONE path, and two is refused with both of
// them named. The alternative a refusal prevents is worse than an error: silently formatting the
// first and ignoring the second reads, on stdout, exactly like a successful run.
//
test 'a second path is refused rather than silently ignored'
	try checkFixture("two-paths-refused")
end 'a second path is refused rather than silently ignored'

// unlexable-file-left-alone.test.maxon — a source the lexer rejects is left BYTE-IDENTICAL and
// counted as unchanged, and the run still succeeds: one unformattable file in a tree must not
// stop the others from being formatted, and must not be half-rewritten from a token stream that
// stopped early.
//
// ⚠ `tests/README.md` records the limit this case cannot cover: `unchanged` means both "already
// perfect" and "could not be read", and nothing in the report tells the two apart. One fixture
// pins it for one file; nothing says it is happening to two hundred.
//
test 'a file the lexer rejects is left byte-identical while its neighbour is formatted'
	try checkFixture("unlexable-file-left-alone")
end 'a file the lexer rejects is left byte-identical while its neighbour is formatted'
// ============================================================================================
// REAL SOURCES, AT SCALE
// ============================================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **THE CORPUS ABOVE HAS ONE STRUCTURAL BLIND SPOT AND IT IS SCALE.** The formatter's indent
// level, block stack and group counters are MONOTONIC across a file: they are carried from the first
// token to the last, so an imbalance does not produce a wrong answer on one line, it produces drift
// that grows. Every fixture above is a few dozen lines at nesting depth 2-3, where such an imbalance
// moves three lines and reads as a layout opinion. The measured incident moved 2,189 lines in a real
// file. This is the one case that reaches real sources.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT STAGES FROM THE LIVE TREE AT TEST TIME AND COMMITS NO COPY.** A committed duplicate of an
// 8,000-line compiler source would drift from the original the first time either was edited, and the
// test would then be measuring a file the compiler no longer has. That is a deliberate exception to
// this directory's "nothing stored here is a live `.maxon`" rule, and it is safe for that rule's own
// reason INVERTED: the rule exists because `maxon fmt` over the checkout would rewrite a stored source
// and silently re-bless the expectation minted from it — and **nothing is stored here, so there is no
// expectation for `fmt` to re-bless.** The roster names paths; it is not Maxon and the walk that
// guards this directory does not read it as a source.
//
// ⭐ **SO EVERY ASSERTION IS DERIVED RATHER THAN PINNED, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF EVERY CASE ABOVE.**
// There is no `expected-tree/` and no `expected-stdout.txt`, and there could not be: the oracle would
// have to be re-minted on every edit to any of the sources, which is a re-bless per commit and a gate
// that means nothing by the second one. What is asserted instead is what must hold for ANY correctly
// formatted version of ANY of these files — every comment line still a comment line, every doc comment
// still a doc comment, every line of code still there, and a second run with nothing left to do.
//
// ⛔ **THE COPY IS FORMATTED, NEVER THE ORIGINAL.** `fmt` is spawned with its working directory in
// gitignored `temp/`, and nothing here passes it a path inside the checkout's source tree.

// Where the roster lives, relative to the checkout root. It is NOT under `fixtures/`: it has no
// `input/`, no `expected-tree/` and no pins, so filed among the case directories it would be counted
// as a case and read as a half-written fixture.
let CensusSourceListFileName = "tests/fmt/census-sources.txt"

// A roster line that is a note rather than a path. The roster explains itself in place, because which
// files are on it and why is the first thing a reader of a failure here needs.
let CensusNotePrefix = "#"

// Where the copies are built. A second staging root beside `StagingRootDirName` rather than a case
// directory under it: `purgeFilesIn` clears a whole root, and sharing one with the fixtures would have
// this test delete a case's staged tree out from under it.
let CensusStagingDirName = "temp/fmt-census"

// The path `fmt` is given: the staging directory it is already running in. Explicit rather than
// relying on the no-argument default, which is a case of its own (`no-argument-defaults-to-cwd`) and
// not what is being asked here.
let CensusStagingDirArgument = "."

// The two comment markers the census counts. They are NOT disjoint — a `///` line contains `//` and is
// counted by both — and that is the point: a doc comment demoted to a plain comment moves one count
// and not the other, where a census of "comment lines" alone would read the swap as no change at all.
let CommentMarker = "//"
let DocCommentMarker = "///"

// What a staged file is called: the source's path with its separators folded into one name, so a flat
// staging directory holds sources from anywhere in the tree without two of them colliding. It also
// guarantees no staged file is named `build.maxon`, which the walk skips by name
// (`build-manifest-skipped`).
let StagedNameSeparator = "_"

// Which of the census test's two runs a message is about. The two failures mean different things: the
// first says `fmt` mangled a real source, the second says a re-run touched a tree it had finished with.
let CensusFirstRunLabel = "the first run"
let CensusSecondRunLabel = "the second run"

// How many lines of one kind a file holds. Named for what it measures rather than for the loop.
typealias LineTally = int(0 to u64.max)

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT MUST HOLD FOR ANY CORRECTLY FORMATTED VERSION OF ANY FILE.** The three counts are taken
// together and reported together, because a failure in which two of them moved says something quite
// different from one in which a single count did.
//
// ⚠ **THEY COUNT LINES, NOT COMMENTS, AND THAT IS THE HONEST BOUND FOR A CENSUS OVER ARBITRARY
// SOURCE**: a `//` inside a string literal is counted too. It does not matter, because the question
// asked is INVARIANCE — the identical rule is applied to both texts, and a formatter that neither
// loses nor duplicates content leaves every one of these numbers exactly where it was.
type Census
	let commentLines as LineTally
	let docCommentLines as LineTally
	let nonBlankLines as LineTally

	// ONE walk, so a file of several thousand lines is split into lines once rather than once per count.
	static function of(text String) returns Census
		var comments = 0 as LineTally
		var docComments = 0 as LineTally
		var nonBlank = 0 as LineTally

		for line in text.split(LineFeed) 'eachLine'
			if line.contains(CommentMarker) 'bearsAComment'
				comments = comments + 1
			end 'bearsAComment'

			if line.contains(DocCommentMarker) 'bearsADocComment'
				docComments = docComments + 1
			end 'bearsADocComment'

			if not line.trim().isEmpty() 'saysSomething'
				nonBlank = nonBlank + 1
			end 'saysSomething'
		end 'eachLine'

		return Self{commentLines: comments, docCommentLines: docComments, nonBlankLines: nonBlank}
	end 'of'

	// The whole census as one line, which is what the two sides are compared as: an equality on text
	// puts all three numbers in front of the reader at once, where three separate assertions would stop
	// at the first and hide the SHAPE of the loss.
	function rendered() returns String
		return "{self.commentLines} comment line(s), {self.docCommentLines} doc-comment line(s), {self.nonBlankLines} non-blank line(s)"
	end 'rendered'
end 'Census'

// One roster entry: where it came from, where its copy is, and what it said before `fmt` saw it. The
// three travel together because every assertion compares the last against the second and names the
// first.
type CensusSource
	let origin as String
	let staged as FilePath
	let before as String

	static function create(origin String, staged FilePath, before String) returns CensusSource
		return Self{origin: origin, staged: staged, before: before}
	end 'create'

	// The one question asked of this source once `fmt` has run over its copy. It is a method rather
	// than a loop body reading three fields, because what a failure has to name — the ORIGIN path — is
	// the field a caller outside the type would have to be handed in order to say it.
	function requireNothingWasLost() throws TestFailure
		let observed = Census.of(fileBody(self.staged)).rendered()
		let expected = Census.of(self.before).rendered()
		try Expect.equal(observed, expected: expected, message: "formatting changed what {self.origin} CONTAINS, not merely how it is laid out. A comment line that stopped being one was destroyed; a doc-comment line that stopped being one was demoted; a missing non-blank line is code or prose that is gone. Layout is the formatter's to change, and not one of these three is layout.")
	end 'requireNothingWasLost'
end 'CensusSource'

typealias CensusSourceArray = Array with CensusSource

test 'formatting real sources keeps every comment, every doc comment and every line of code'
	let sources = stageEveryCensusSource()

	let firstRun = spawnFmtOverCensusTree()
	try requireCensusRunSucceeded(firstRun, runLabel: CensusFirstRunLabel)

	for source in sources 'eachSource'
		try source.requireNothingWasLost()
	end 'eachSource'

	// ⭐ Idempotence over real sources is a different claim from idempotence over a fixture: a rule that
	// flips a construct between two spellings needs that construct to appear, and the cases above reach
	// a few dozen constructs where these files reach thousands.
	//
	// The prediction goes through `summaryLine` — the ONE grammar this file parses the fixtures' first
	// run with and predicts their second run with — rather than a substring: what the second run must
	// say is exactly `0 changed` over every file the walk found, and the walk found what was staged.
	let secondRun = spawnFmtOverCensusTree()
	try requireCensusRunSucceeded(secondRun, runLabel: CensusSecondRunLabel)

	let observedStdout = secondRun.stdout.replace(CarriageReturn, with: "")
	// ⚠ No `as FileTally` on the count: shv2 accepts the cast, the BOOTSTRAP rejects it as
	// `E3010 unneeded cast` — and this corpus has to compile under BOTH, because running it under the
	// reference is what closes the circularity for a driver that tests itself.
	let expectedStdout = "{summaryLine(IdempotentChangedCount, unchanged: sources.count())}{LineFeed}"
	try Expect.equal(observedStdout, expected: expectedStdout, message: "a second `{TestedCommandName}` over the tree the first one left did not report that it had nothing to do. Formatting a formatted file must be a fixed point; anything else means the tool moves a real source a little further every time it is run over it.")
end 'formatting real sources keeps every comment, every doc comment and every line of code'

// ⛔ **AN EMPTY ROSTER IS A PANIC, NOT A PASS.** A test that formatted nothing reports exactly what a
// test that formatted everything and agreed reports — and unlike every case above, this one has no
// stored expectation whose absence would give it away.
function stageEveryCensusSource() returns CensusSourceArray
	let stagingRoot = writableDir(CensusStagingDirName)
	purgeFilesIn(stagingRoot)

	var staged = CensusSourceArray.create()
	for origin in censusRosterPaths() 'eachOrigin'
		let body = fileBody(censusSourcePath(origin))
		let target = stagingRoot.join("{stagedCensusNameFor(origin)}")
		try File.writeText(target, content: body) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — could not stage a copy of {origin} at {target.toString()}. The ORIGINAL is never formatted, so without the copy there is nothing to format.")

		staged.push(CensusSource.create(origin, staged: target, before: body))
	end 'eachOrigin'

	if staged.isEmpty() 'nothingStaged'
		panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — {CensusSourceListFileName} names no source, so `{TestedCommandName}` would run over an empty directory and this test would pass having formatted nothing.")
	end 'nothingStaged'

	return staged
end 'stageEveryCensusSource'

// The roster with its notes and blank lines dropped. It is read from a file rather than written here
// so that the reason each source is on the list sits beside the path, where whoever adds one reads it.
function censusRosterPaths() returns StringArray
	let path = try FilePath.from(CensusSourceListFileName) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — '{CensusSourceListFileName}' is not a spellable path")
	let text = try File.readText(path) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — could not read {CensusSourceListFileName}. Every path in this file is relative to the CHECKOUT ROOT (see the header); a run started anywhere else finds nothing.")

	var paths = StringArray.create()
	for line in text.split(LineFeed) 'eachLine'
		let entry = line.trim()
		if entry.isEmpty() or entry.startsWith(CensusNotePrefix) 'aNote'
			continue
		end 'aNote'

		paths.push(entry)
	end 'eachLine'

	return paths
end 'censusRosterPaths'

// A source the roster names, refused loudly when it is not there. A file that has been moved or
// renamed must BREAK this test rather than quietly shrink it: a roster that silently lost an entry
// formats less every time the tree moves, and says nothing.
function censusSourcePath(spelled String) returns FilePath
	let path = try FilePath.from(spelled) otherwise panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — '{spelled}' is not a spellable path")
	if not File.exists(path) 'notThere'
		panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — {CensusSourceListFileName} names '{spelled}', and there is no file there. Point the roster at the file's new home, with the note saying why it is on the list.")
	end 'notThere'

	return path
end 'censusSourcePath'

// A source's path folded into one staging filename. See `StagedNameSeparator`.
function stagedCensusNameFor(origin String) returns String
	return origin.replace(PortablePathSeparator, with: StagedNameSeparator)
end 'stagedCensusNameFor'

// Spawn the compiler under test over the census staging tree.
function spawnFmtOverCensusTree() returns CollectedOutput
	var arguments = StringArray.create()
	arguments.push(TestedCommandName)
	arguments.push(CensusStagingDirArgument)

	return spawnCompiler(arguments, workingDirectory: writableDir(CensusStagingDirName), what: "the real-sources census")
end 'spawnFmtOverCensusTree'

// ⛔⛔ **THE ONE SPAWN, AND EVERY CALLER GOES THROUGH IT.** Three of them — a fixture case, the
// option-ahead-of-command probe, and the real-sources census — and what they share is not the six
// lines: it is the CONTRACT that a missing binary or a spawn that never happened is the GATE being
// broken, never `fmt` answering wrongly. Three copies of that judgement would drift into three
// different verdicts about the same non-event, and a reader sent looking for a formatter defect would
// find nothing there. What actually differs between the callers is the argv, the directory and the
// noun a message names, so those are the parameters and nothing else is.
//
// ⛔ It PANICS rather than failing an assertion, for that reason.
function spawnCompiler(arguments StringArray, workingDirectory FilePath, what String) returns CollectedOutput
	let binary = testedCompilerPath()
	if not File.exists(binary) 'noBinary'
		panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — there is no compiler at {binary.toString()}, which {what} needs. This file's paths are relative to the CHECKOUT ROOT (see the header); run it from there, and build `{TestedCompilerStem}` first.")
	end 'noBinary'

	return try Subprocess.run(Executable.path(binary), arguments: arguments, workingDirectory: workingDirectory) otherwise (e) 'spawnFailed'
		panic("fmt-fixtures.test: the gate could not run — could not spawn {what} ({e.displayReason()}). It pins what `{TestedCommandName}` reports AND what it writes, and a tree nothing compares is a claim.")
	end 'spawnFailed'
end 'spawnCompiler'

// A run that REFUSED wrote nothing, so every census would agree and a `fmt` that failed outright would
// pass as a green. The status is therefore asked before anything is compared.
function requireCensusRunSucceeded(run CollectedOutput, runLabel String) throws TestFailure
	let observed = run.exitCode()
	let expected = SuccessExitCode
	try Expect.equal(observed, expected: expected, message: "`{TestedCommandName}` did not succeed on {runLabel} over the real sources staged from the tree. A run that refused wrote nothing, so every census this test takes would agree and the failure would read as a pass. It wrote to stderr: {run.stderr}")
end 'requireCensusRunSucceeded'
